From ebaaf97669d9cad517cde955f75fd7e8bded9586 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: EvilPatrick06 <187923600+EvilPatrick06@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 18:20:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(webhook): a coalesced delivery survives a gateway restart

A coalesced delivery is acknowledged (202 "coalesced") before any admission,
and a provider does not resend an acknowledged delivery, so the coalescer's
buffer is the event's only copy. On a stop, drain_gateway_runtime withdraws
every adapter's message handler before the adapters disconnect, so the
webhook adapter's disconnect flush admitted each pending group against a
withdrawn ingress: producer_scope found no runner and raised
RuntimeStoreError('not_found'), the group was logged as "not admitted" and
was gone. A hard kill lost the buffer outright.

Each pending group is now also written to a spool in the gateway's home
(webhook_coalesce_pending.json, 0600, atomic) until its admission commits.
route_config is never written (it can hold the route's HMAC secret); the
group keeps route_name, event_type and profile, and the route is read back
from the live routes. While ingress is withdrawn (no runner, no session
authority, or draining) a settled group is not tried: it stays spooled.
When the next run's listener is up and its ingress wired, every spooled
group is buffered again with a fresh quiet window and admitted as usual. A
group whose route is gone or no longer coalesces is dropped and named; an
unreadable spool is set aside, never overwritten. A refusal by a serving
gateway behaves as before: logged, not kept, and the batch's delivery ids
given back so a resend runs.
